CHAPTER ONE
1.0 Introduction
Agriculture plays a significant role in enhancing food security, employment creation, and income generation in Kenya. Livestock production, particularly poultry farming, contributes substantially to household nutrition and economic development. However, farmers in many parts of Kenya continue to face numerous challenges, including high feed costs, disease outbreaks, water scarcity, climate variability, and unstable market prices.
As part of the Kenya Certificate of Secondary Education (KCSE) Agriculture 433/3 Project, candidates are required to analyze their locality, identify prevailing agricultural challenges, and design a sustainable livestock enterprise that addresses these challenges while ensuring profitability, environmental conservation, and improved production efficiency.
St. Mary's Girls Secondary School – Thigio is located in Ndeiya Sub-County, Kiambu County, an area where poultry keeping is a common enterprise among households and small-scale farmers. Following a locality analysis and market survey, the Agriculture candidates identified indigenous chicken broiler production as a suitable enterprise due to its high market demand, adaptability to local conditions, low capital requirements, and potential for value addition.
The project involved twenty-four (24) candidates working in five groups to establish and manage a climate-smart poultry enterprise aimed at producing high-quality indigenous chicken meat while addressing challenges associated with feed costs, diseases, climate change, and market fluctuations.
